determined nominees keeps uncertainty high and leaves the state a genuine tossup with a slight Republican edge.", "watch": "Early general-election polling or the emergence of nominees with unusual crossover appeal could quickly move this narrow margin in either direction.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "North Carolina remains one of the closest presidential battlegrounds, with a slight Republican lean at the state level. The incumbent president is term-limited, leaving an open race in which neither party's nominee is yet known. No general-election polls, expert ratings, or market prices are available for this contest, so the forecast rests on the state's fundamentals: a partisan lean of about one point toward Republicans and a recent history of narrow Republican wins in presidential years.\n\nBecause both parties' nominees are undetermined, candidate quality and campaign dynamics could move the state in either direction. North Carolina's growing urban and suburban populations have kept Democrats competitive even as rural areas trend Republican. The race stands as a genuine tossup with a slight Republican edge, and uncertainty is high given the absence of direct polling this far out.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4508, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4639, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.4659, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.4636, "margin": -1.0}]}